### Page 6 of 9

```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
January 6, 2026
Land Use:Variances/Board App./Plats
18. Consideration of the cancellation of a PUBLIC HEARING regarding
the modest subdivision permit and plat for Walden Ranch Estates, 4th
Filing, Laramie County, WY.
26 - 014
Cancellation of Modest Plat, Walden Ranch Estates,
4th Filiing
Attachments:
Justin Arnold, Planning, said the applicant informed the project will not be completed
and requested the application be withdrawn. No board action is required.
The executive summary was received and filed.
19. PUBLIC HEARING regarding the Project Jade site plan and the BFC
Power and Cheyenne Power Hub Project site plan located in Sects 5
and 7, T12N, R66W, of the 6th PM, Laramie County, WY.
26 - 023
Resolutions, Power and Cheyenne Power Hub Project
Site Plans, Revised
Project Jade and BFC Power and Cheyenne Power
Hub Project Site Plans
Attachments:
Justin Arnold, Planning, said Tallgrass Integrated Solutions, LLC, and WY DC 1, LLC,
submitted applications for the Project Jade site plan and the BFC Power and
Cheyenne Power Hub Project site plan. He said the projects require two separate
board actions.
Arnold said the two projects were combined into a single report for ease of
readability. The purpose of the projects is to develop a data center (Project Jade) and
a power plant (BFC Power and Cheyenne Power Hub Project) within the newly formed
Switchgrass Industrial Park. He said board approval is requested because the
projects have significant impact on the surrounding area. Arnold said Project Jade's
site will be developed on Sect 5, a 600-acre parcel, and will consist of five data center
buildings, two support buildings, and associated infrastructure. He said the BFC
Power and Cheyenne Power Hub's site will be developed on Sect 7, a 659-acre
parcel, and will consist of two power generation facilities and associated
infrastructure.
Raymon Williams, Tallgrass, Matt Field and Sara Axelrod, Crusoe, spoke about their
respective companies and projects. They noted the various mitigation efforts that will
be undertaken, including limiting truck traffic in residential areas, acquiring water from
the Lance Formation aquifer, and opening a local office with a dedicated liaison to
answer resident questions and help with issues. They said the projects represent a
significant investment in Laramie and will help move the country forward by making it
more competitive as an AI leader. They said the projects are being developed with the
community in mind to maximize benefits and minimize impacts.
Page 6 of 9
```

### Page 7 of 9

```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
January 6, 2026
Arnold said based on evidence provided, staff recommends approval of both site
plans with the adoption of findings of fact a and b with four conditions: 1) All agency
comments will be addressed, and/or corrected prior to the issuance of a Certificate of
Review by Laramie County Planning and Development, 2) Access and Drainage
easements will be recorded with the County Clerk's office prior to the issuance of a
Certificate of Review by Laramie County Planning and Development, 3) Legal access
to the property must be established and 4) Approval from WYDOT is required.
Chairman Malm opened the hearing for PUBLIC COMMENT.
Randy Fox, Hyndman Heights HOA, expressed concerns about traffic congestion,
questioned whether there is a sufficient survey of the Lance Formation, creation of a
man camp, and asked whether there is a need for future review if the power plant
increases in size. Fox expressed that the companies have been responsive and have
demonstrated good neighborliness, and that some concerns have been alleviated
through the review of the staff report.
Amber Leyba, Greater Cheyenne Chamber of Commerce; Senator Evie Brennan,
SD31; Chief of Staff Drew Perkins, Governor's office; Riley McCauley and Noah
Loetscher, Western States Regional Council of Carpenters; Betsy Hale, LEADS;
Pete Obermueller, Petroleum Association of Wyoming; Tyler McLachlan and Tucker
Fagan, residents, support the projects. Comments included the positive economic
impact of projects which help Laramie County remain competitive, opportunities for
young people to stay in Wyoming, national security, advancements and the need to
win the AI race, closed-loop data center using water, self-power generation,
expansion of additional core industries, creation of jobs and return on investments.
Ryan Cox, State Engineer's office, said he believes the applicants will be 100 percent
within the Lance Formation when the projects are completed.
Commissioner Heath expressed concerns about the impact of workers staying in
hotels, the limitation on tourists' ability to get rooms, and decreased revenue from
lodging taxes when workers remain for more than 30 days.
Thomas White and Michelle Jessica, residents, expressed concerns about
contamination and consumption of groundwater, traffic, lighting, changes to the
viewshed, and the need to fund the educational system.
Hearing no further PUBLIC COMMENT, Chairman Malm closed the hearing.
A motion was made by Vice Chairman Thompson, seconded by
Commissioner Hollingshead, to grant approval of the Project Jade site
plan permit, and adopt the findings of fact a and b of the staff report with
the following conditions: 1) All agency comments will be addressed, and/or
corrected prior to the issuance of a Certificate of Review by Laramie
Page 7 of 9
```

