```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
January 6, 2026
County Planning and Development, 2) Access and Drainage easements
will be recorded with the County Clerk's office prior to the issuance of a
Certificate of Review by Laramie County Planning and Development, 3)
Legal access to the property must be established and 4) Approval from
WYDOT is required. The motion carried by the following vote:
Malm, Thompson, Heath, Hollingshead,
Zwonitzer
Aye:
A motion was made by Vice Chairman Thompson, seconded by
Commissioner Hollingshead, to grant approval of the BFC Power and
Cheyenne Power Hub Project site plan permit, and adopt the findings of
fact a and b of the staff report with the following conditions: 1) All agency
comments will be addressed and/or corrected prior to the issuance of a
Certificate of Review by Laramie County Planning and Development, 2)
Access and Drainage easements will be recorded with the County Clerk's
office prior to the issuance of a Certificate of Review by Laramie County
Planning and Development, 3) Legal access to the property must be
established and 4) Approval from WYDOT is required.
Commissioner Thompson said if residents have concerns, they should come and talk
with the board. He noted he feels the applicants will be good neighbors, as
demonstrated by Fox's testimony.
Commissioner Zwonitzer echoed the sentiments regarding stakeholder engagement
and due diligence. He spoke about how Tallgrass will use Wyoming's legacy
industries. Commissioner Zwonitzer said the power plant will be located out of sight
and will have a low visual impact.
Commissioner Heath said there will be pros and cons to every project or change
coming into the community. She spoke about property taxes and reinvesting in
education, which are under the state legislature's control. Commissioner Heath
discussed the benefits, noting that applicants will pay property and sales taxes, which
will benefit education and county services.
Commissioner Hollingshead said, as a father and grandfather, one thing he considers
in evaluating developments in Laramie County is keeping our young people here, and
he wants it to be a multi-generational community.
Chairman Malm spoke about private property rights, mitigating risks and ensuring
public safety. He expressed hope that the legislature will fund education at the level
needed. On the subject of water,  he said the applicants will use significantly less
water than if the land were a subdivision. He spoke about the county's future role at the
forefront of the next arms race in AI and compared it to our role in nuclear deterrence.
